 On the analytical side the contribution is a measurement layer and a set of bounded negatives. The Zero-Variance Fraction is a reproducible diagnostic of advantage-signal starvation, recomputable from stored tensors, regime-dependent in magnitude (0.72–0.77 on the Phase-1 main configuration, 0.16 to 0.84 across the measured runs; Table 1.1), with an association with final held-out outcome that is not distinguishable from zero (ρ = 0.27, 95% CI [−0.37, 0.88], n = 23). It is descriptive, not predictive. Its theory has been made falsifiable, and two of its three consequences reproduced: the U-shape in accuracy across reward levels and the monotone group-size effect; the gradient link remains toy-scale and directional (source: platform_hybrid/paper/sections/p7_abstract.tex). Group size therefore has a candidate mechanism, but the measured three-seed sweep found its arms statistically indistinguishable and establishes no optimum (Chapter 6 §6.4). Length bias is not established: the roster-level flag that marks four of eleven GRPO runs — peak before 65% of training, terminal reward below 90% of peak (source: LIMITATIONS_AND_IMPACT.md §2.2) — has post hoc thresholds, is not specific to length, and fires on most runs in the wider trace pool, so it is not evidence of length bias; in the controlled comparison the same rule fires on both GRPO and Dr. GRPO (five of six GSM8K-CoT runs) while no run shows length growing, completion length drifts shorter, and the two are indistinguishable on held-out gain (Chapter 6 §6.5). No claim is made that Dr. GRPO is uniquely free of the pattern. The levers we could test — a naive curriculum, a matched-token-budget intervention, an integer group-size sweep — did not produce reliable held-out gains. The earlier same-stack de-confounds (PPO versus GRPO, GRPO versus Dr. GRPO, and group size) were all measured on a saturated 0.5B arithmetic task with evaluation accuracy near 0.99, so their nulls say that no difference was detectable there, not 